 in \\boxed{...}.\n\nFind the minimum possible value of\n\\[\\sqrt{58 - 42x} + \\sqrt{149 - 140 \\sqrt{1 - x^2}}\\]where $-1 \\le x \\le 1.$","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"69017ffbb928be77757664ebe7e15063951a324b777ed1033682b4afbddeea4d"},"seconds":54.50051808357239,"seed":150112482270889792,"stop":"eos","text":"To find the minimum value of the expression\n\\[\\sqrt{58 - 42x} + \\sqrt{149 - 140 \\sqrt{1 - x^2}},\\]\nwe start by analyzing the individual terms and their behavior with respect to \\(x\\).\n\nFirst, let's denote the expression as:\n\\[f(x) = \\sqrt{58 - 42x} + \\sqrt{149 - 140 \\sqrt{1 - x^2}}.\\]\n\n### Step 1: Consider the endpoints\nEvaluate \\(f(x)\\) at the endpoints of the interval \\([-1, 1]\\).\n\n#### At \\(x = -1\\):\n\\[\nf(-1) = \\sqrt{58 - 42(-1)} + \\sqrt{149 - 140 \\sqrt{1 - (-1)^2}}.\n\\]\nSimplify inside the square roots:\n\\[\nf(-1) = \\sqrt{58 + 42} + \\sqrt{149 - 140 \\sqrt{1 - 1}} = \\sqrt{100} + \\sqrt{149 - 140 \\cdot 0} = 10 + \\sqrt{149}.\n\\]\n\n#### At \\(x = 1\\):\n\\[\nf(1) = \\sqrt{58 - 42(1)} + \\sqrt{149 - 140 \\sqrt{1 - 1^2}}.\n\\]\nSimplify inside the square roots:\n\\[\nf(1) = \\sqrt{58 - 42} + \\sqrt{149 - 140 \\sqrt{0}} = \\sqrt{16} + \\sqrt{149} = 4 + \\sqrt{149}.\n\\]\n\n### Step 2: Consider critical points\nTo find critical points, we need to take the derivative of \\(f(x)\\) and set it to zero. However, this approach can be complex due to the nature of the function. Instead, we will look for a more insightful approach.\n\n### Step 3: Symmetry and trigonometric substitution\nConsider the substitution \\(x = \\cos \\theta\\), where \\(\\theta \\in [0, \\pi]\\). Then \\(\\sqrt{1 - x^2} = \\sin \\theta\\).\n\nRewrite the expression:\n\\[\nf(\\cos \\theta) = \\sqrt{58 - 42 \\cos \\theta} + \\sqrt{149 - 140 \\sin \\theta}.\n\\]\n\n### Step 4: Evaluate at specific angles\nEvaluate at \\(\\theta = \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\) (i.e., \\(x = 0\\)):\n\\[\nf(0) = \\sqrt{58 - 42 \\cdot 0} + \\sqrt{149 - 140 \\cdot 1} = \\sqrt{58} + \\sqrt{9} = \\sqrt{58} + 3.\n\\]\n\n### Step 5: Compare values\nWe have:\n\\[\nf(-1) = 10 + \\sqrt{149}, \\quad f(1) = 4 + \\sqrt{149}, \\quad f(0) = \\sqrt{58} + 3.\n\\]\n\n### Step 6: Find the minimum\nAmong these values:\n\\[\n10 + \\sqrt{149} \\approx 10 + 12.20656 = 22.20656,\n\\]\n\\[\n4 + \\sqrt{149} \\approx 4 + 12.20656 = 16.20656,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\sqrt{58} + 3 \\approx 7.61577 + 3 = 10.61577.\n\\]\n\nThus, the minimum value is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{10.61577}.\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{10.61577}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
